Z||000000||OilStumped||05-01-99||10:18 PM||||I got problems....my 85 GT leaks 3 quarts of oil a week...it SUCKS! it blows all up on the taillight panel and stuff....yuck.  I have no clue where its coming from.  I changed the oil pan gasket...but to no avail.  I really need some advice.  
Z||000001||EMF||05-02-99||12:00 AM||||HAHAHAHAHAHAHAHAHAHA<br>sorry.<br>
Z||000002||PaUL||05-02-99||12:08 AM||||Sounds like your engine has high internal pressure. After an engine wears down, blow-by gets pass the rings and pressurizes the inside of the block and heads. The oil is probably being forced out between the valve cover and head. If you change the gasket and oil still gets out, youll probably have to rebuild the engine. Another cause could be a plugged breather or pcv valve. When you change the valve cover gaskets, its important not to overtighten, if you do it'll leak for sure.
Z||000003||OilStumped||05-02-99||09:00 AM||||Thanks paul.  Thats what I thought the problem was...great...now I get to pull the engine again.  Maybe I should just take the motor from my other one...beef it up a little...and shove it in.  hmmm...or maybe a 3.1 would be nice...haha!  Nah....I think the valve cover gaskets can be changed by just pulling the intake.  I think your right about the high pressure thing, cause when its cold the oil press. gauge is almost pegged...then it settles back down once it gets warm.  Thanks for the help!
Z||000004||PaUL||05-02-99||01:38 PM||||Another place the oil could be shooting out of is the base of the distributor. If there is a pool of oil in cavity next to it on the block, you need to change the rubber O ring on the dist shaft. Oil could also be cumming out the dipstick tube. 
Z||000005||Patrick||05-02-99||02:18 PM||||OilStumped,<p>I think you're <b>totally</b> misinterpreting what Paul is saying to you. You've mentioned that your oil pressure gauge is showing a lot of pressure when the engine is cold, and then it drops as the engine warms up. This is <b>normal</b> (within reason). This is <b>NOT</b> what Paul is talking about. He is describing <b>atmospheric</b> (air) pressure in the crankcase, <b>not oil pressure</b>. Excessive air pressure in the crankcase can force (vaporized) oil out through any imperfections in gaskets and/or seals.<p>Make sure your <b>Positive Crankcase Ventilation</b> (PCV) system is working properly. It produces a <b>vacuum</b> in the crankcase which helps to stop atmospheric pressure there from building up and forcing oil past gaskets and/or seals.<p>Your engine is probably covered in oil and dirt which makes it difficult to figure out where the heck the oil is leaking from. Why don't you try cleaning your engine with steam or chemicals, and then you'd at least be able to see where the oil is coming from. If it's JUST a faulty gasket or seal, you'll then be able to make the proper repair. <p>If you have more serious problems, well, at least you'll have a nice clean engine to pull out.:)<p>  
Z||000006||ezsurf||05-02-99||08:18 PM||||OilStumped,<br>I think I can tell you where your oil leak is, because I just found mine.  I already pulled the engine, and I knew I was leaking oil around the valve covers.  When I pulled the valve covers, I found the heads looked like they had been packed in bearing grease.  All oil return holes were completely clogged.  The valve cover seals were good, but the oil had no place to go. Oil was being forced out the seals.  I'm looking at a complete engine rebuild.  I already spent four hours cleaning up the engine.  Since the oil had no place to go, it was pooling in the head.  When I pulled the valve cover, it coated the engine all over again.  Yes, I have quickly learned that this engine's a bitch to work on.
Z||000007||Patrick||05-03-99||12:01 AM||||Hey ezsurf,<p>Sounds like your engine would be the perfect poster "child" for companies like Quaker State, Fram, etc. I bet the previous owner of your Fiero never actually <b>changed[b] the oil, he probably just added to it. Meanwhile, all that sludge and crap just continued to build up inside the engine. As much as it's a pain in the butt, I think that you're doing the right thing by pulling the engine and stripping it down. <p>Having said all that, your situation does [b]not</b> necessarily suggest that the problem OilStumped has is the same as yours. There can be many reasons for oil leaks, and an equal number of solutions.<p><p><p>  <p>
Z||000008||Patrick||05-03-99||12:08 AM||||Hey ezsurf,<br> <br>Sounds like your engine would be the perfect poster "child" for companies like Quaker State, Fram, etc. I bet the previous owner of your Fiero never actually <b>changed</b> the oil, he probably just added to it. Meanwhile, all that sludge and crap just continued to build up inside the engine. As much as it's a pain in the butt, I think that you're doing the right thing by pulling the engine and stripping it down.<br> <br>Having said all that, your situation does <b>not</b> necessarily suggest that the problem OilStumped has is the same as yours. There can be many reasons for oil leaks, and an equal number of solutions.<p><br>
