Z||000012||OilStumped||05-04-99||02:26 PM||||Hey thanks a million guys! I did the ole pressure wash trick and it worked...my valve covers leak real bad.  I was at my girlfriends house...drove about 2 miles to the car wash soaked it down and drove back and I could tell right away.  So payday is tomorrow and I'll be buyin an upper engine gasket kit, cause I don't think you can get the cover off the front half without takin the intake off too.  plus I just wanna make sure its all good.  =)  thanks again folks!
Z||000013||ezsurf||05-04-99||06:44 PM||||OilStumped,<br>I can tell you right now that you can't get the valve covers off without pulling the top two sections of the intake manifold.  Someone mentioned you need the E-16 socket to remove the bolts in the manifold.  I did not have this, but I confirmed the fact that you can use a standard socket.  However, it must be a 12 point socket.  I used a 10mm 12 point socket, and I also needed an 8mm 12 point socket.  One of the bolts is smaller (for your convenience.)  I think you have the same problem I do.  Don't be surprised if you have to do a complete engine rebuild.  (Misery loves company.)  
Z||000014||PaUL||05-05-99||02:09 PM||||Ive seen 2 different types of valve cover gaskets for the 2.8. One is made of cork and the other some kind of rubber. Get the rubber if you can, I think it lasts longer. <p>As I said before, DO NOT overtighten, if you do the head will cut into the gasket and it will leak again. Ive had lots of problems with my valve covers leaking. I use the rubber gaskets and in addition use silicone to hold the gasket in place while tightening and to provide a better seal. <p>You should also check your breather, this is a fitting on the bulkhead side head. Its connected to a hose going to the air cleaner box. If this is plugged, it will cause pressure to build up in the crankcase forcing oil past the gaskets.
